1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,258
In Gull Harbor, it rains two hundred days a year.

2
00:00:06,308 --> 00:00:09,241
Drizzle was ready for every one of them.

3
00:00:12,041 --> 00:00:15,896
He was ready for rain. He was not ready for this.

4
00:00:16,096 --> 00:00:20,850
At 10:42 on a Tuesday morning, his coffee cup started to shiver.

5
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:23,138
Then the floor moved.

6
00:00:23,338 --> 00:00:26,429
Every instinct told him to run for the doorway.

7
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:28,045
That's an old myth.

8
00:00:28,095 --> 00:00:35,317
In a modern house, a doorway is no stronger than any other part of the wall, and it's right where things fall on you.

9
00:00:35,517 --> 00:00:37,961
So he did the thing that works.

10
00:00:40,761 --> 00:00:43,999
Under the table. One hand gripping a leg.

11
00:00:44,049 --> 00:00:46,131
One arm over his head.

12
00:00:46,331 --> 00:00:49,452
Mags from next door was already under there with him.

13
00:00:49,502 --> 00:00:52,981
She'd come over for coffee. She was staying for this.

14
00:00:53,181 --> 00:00:58,761
Ten seconds. Twenty. A minute. Small earthquakes end fast.

15
00:00:58,811 --> 00:01:00,197
This one didn't.

16
00:01:02,997 --> 00:01:05,325
The shaking lasted four minutes.

17
00:01:05,375 --> 00:01:08,228
Long enough to know exactly what it was.

18
00:01:08,278 --> 00:01:13,513
The Cascadia fault, seven hundred miles of it, had finally let go.

19
00:01:13,713 --> 00:01:15,400
Then the lights went out.

20
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:23,125
When the shaking stopped, the kitchen was full of broken glass and one very loud question.

21
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:24,514
Now what?

22
00:01:24,714 --> 00:01:27,064
Gull Harbor sits right on the coast.

23
00:01:27,114 --> 00:01:30,915
And on the coast, a quake that long means one thing.

24
00:01:30,965 --> 00:01:32,722
The ocean is coming.

25
00:01:33,072 --> 00:01:35,622
MAGS: "We're not waiting for a siren."

26
00:01:35,722 --> 00:01:39,946
She was right. On the coast, the shaking is the warning.

27
00:01:39,996 --> 00:01:42,216
There may never be a siren.

28
00:01:42,416 --> 00:01:46,828
Drizzle grabbed his laptop. His photo album. A plant.

29
00:01:46,878 --> 00:01:49,039
Then he put them all down.

30
00:01:51,839 --> 00:01:57,063
The go-bag by the door had everything that mattered. Water.

31
00:01:57,113 --> 00:02:00,474
A flashlight. A radio. Medicine. A warm layer.

32
00:02:00,524 --> 00:02:03,649
Packed months ago, for exactly this.

33
00:02:03,699 --> 00:02:05,987
It took three seconds to grab.

34
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:10,644
Outside, Gull Harbor looked wrong. Windows shattered.

35
00:02:10,694 --> 00:02:12,307
Power lines down.

36
00:02:12,357 --> 00:02:15,292
The street cracked open like a dropped plate.

37
00:02:15,492 --> 00:02:18,453
And down at the harbor, something stranger.

38
00:02:18,503 --> 00:02:20,366
The water was leaving.

39
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:27,138
Boats sat in the mud. Fish flopped on the sand.

40
00:02:27,188 --> 00:02:29,299
A few people walked out to look.

41
00:02:29,349 --> 00:02:32,185
That's the deadliest mistake on the coast.

42
00:02:32,235 --> 00:02:35,623
When the sea pulls back, it comes back bigger.

43
00:02:35,823 --> 00:02:40,060
Cars were already jammed on Harbor Way. Horns blaring.

44
00:02:40,110 --> 00:02:41,716
Going nowhere.

45
00:02:44,516 --> 00:02:47,122
Cracked roads. Fallen lines. Gridlock.

46
00:02:47,172 --> 00:02:50,495
Feet don't get stuck in traffic.

47
00:02:50,545 --> 00:02:53,072
Drizzle and Mags walked uphill.

48
00:02:53,272 --> 00:02:58,956
The evacuation trail was steep, muddy, and exactly where the signs said it would be.

49
00:03:01,756 --> 00:03:06,457
Point four miles to the hilltop. On a normal day, ten minutes.

50
00:03:06,507 --> 00:03:08,928
With aftershocks, longer.

51
00:03:09,128 --> 00:03:11,851
Halfway up, Mags' knees gave out.

52
00:03:11,901 --> 00:03:15,413
Drizzle gave her his shoulder. They kept moving.

53
00:03:17,463 --> 00:03:22,213
RADIO: "…tsunami warning for the entire Washington coast… move to high ground immediately…"

54
00:03:22,313 --> 00:03:23,472
Official word, finally.

55
00:03:23,522 --> 00:03:29,407
But by then, everyone who'd listened to the shaking was already climbing.

56
00:03:29,607 --> 00:03:30,912
Drizzle looked back.

57
00:03:30,962 --> 00:03:34,387
A gray line stretched across the whole horizon.

58
00:03:34,437 --> 00:03:36,152
It wasn't a cloud.

59
00:03:38,952 --> 00:03:43,875
Twenty-two minutes after the shaking stopped, the first wave hit Gull Harbor.

60
00:03:43,925 --> 00:03:48,058
It took the dock. The ferry. Then Main Street.

61
00:03:48,258 --> 00:03:52,060
Drizzle wanted to stop and watch. Mags didn't let him.

62
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:57,306
Tsunamis come in waves, for hours.

63
00:03:57,356 --> 00:04:01,235
The first one is often not the biggest.

64
00:04:01,285 --> 00:04:04,592
You don't go back down until officials say it's safe.

65
00:04:05,392 --> 00:04:09,253
The hilltop was cold, crowded, and above the water.

66
00:04:09,303 --> 00:04:11,311
That was all that mattered.

67
00:04:11,511 --> 00:04:15,137
Town volunteers had a tarp up and a check-in table running.

68
00:04:15,187 --> 00:04:19,493
The school had walked every kid up the hill. They'd practiced.

69
00:04:19,693 --> 00:04:21,980
Drizzle counted what he'd done right.

70
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:25,274
One. Drop, cover, hold on. Two.

71
00:04:25,324 --> 00:04:30,914
On the coast, the shaking is your warning. Walk uphill, now. Three.

72
00:04:30,964 --> 00:04:34,630
Grab the go-bag, not your stuff.

73
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:37,561
And four. He'd brought snacks.

74
00:04:37,761 --> 00:04:39,866
Then the radio spoke again.

75
00:04:40,216 --> 00:04:44,966
RADIO: "…Highway 101 bridge at Gull Creek has collapsed… coastal roads closed…"

76
00:04:45,066 --> 00:04:47,264
The only road out was gone.

77
00:04:47,314 --> 00:04:51,228
The whole coast had been hit at once. Help would come.

78
00:04:51,278 --> 00:04:54,354
Just not today. Maybe not this week.

79
00:04:54,704 --> 00:04:57,254
DRIZZLE: "So… nobody's coming?"

80
00:04:57,354 --> 00:05:01,825
Below them, the water was still rising. Night was coming.

81
00:05:01,875 --> 00:05:07,061
And somewhere down in the dark, a light flickered on where no one should be.
